WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.611 --> 00:00:03.693
[Narrator] A turtle's shell
is as much a part of its body

00:00:03.693 --> 00:00:06.280
as our rib cage is of ours.

00:00:06.280 --> 00:00:09.471
In fact, it is their rib cage,

00:00:09.471 --> 00:00:10.938
and their spine,

00:00:10.938 --> 00:00:12.471
and their vertebrae,

00:00:12.471 --> 00:00:14.379
and their sternum.

00:00:14.379 --> 00:00:18.060
Basically, a turtle's
skeleton is inside out.

00:00:18.060 --> 00:00:20.115
And just like you can't take a skeleton

00:00:20.115 --> 00:00:23.420
out of a person, right,
you can't take a turtle

00:00:23.420 --> 00:00:25.710
out of its shell either.

00:00:25.710 --> 00:00:27.310
But if you could,

00:00:27.310 --> 00:00:30.431
you'd probably be surprised
by what you'd discover.

00:00:31.820 --> 00:00:33.573
Maria Wojakowski: Here's
the inside of a turtle.

00:00:33.573 --> 00:00:35.410
[Narrator] That's Maria Wojakowski,

00:00:35.410 --> 00:00:38.520
a biologist who's been
studying turtle ecology

00:00:38.520 --> 00:00:40.660
for more than a decade.

00:00:40.660 --> 00:00:42.709
Wojakowski: Here's your shoulder girdle.

00:00:42.709 --> 00:00:44.443
Here's your hip girdle.

00:00:44.443 --> 00:00:46.610
[Narrator] Notice how
those hips and shoulders

00:00:46.610 --> 00:00:50.290
are actually inside the turtle's rib cage?

00:00:50.290 --> 00:00:53.770
Turtles are one of the only
land animals on the planet

00:00:53.770 --> 00:00:55.410
with this feature.

00:00:55.410 --> 00:00:58.739
They're also some of the
only animals that can breathe

00:00:58.739 --> 00:01:00.430
with their butts.

00:01:00.430 --> 00:01:02.280
You see, inside a turtle shell

00:01:02.280 --> 00:01:06.080
is a very particular respiratory system.

00:01:06.080 --> 00:01:08.830
Wojakowski: You will see the
lungs towards the top here.

00:01:08.830 --> 00:01:11.462
[Narrator] Now, most land animals breathe

00:01:11.462 --> 00:01:14.888
by expanding and contracting their ribs,

00:01:14.888 --> 00:01:17.850
which creates a natural
pump that guides air

00:01:17.850 --> 00:01:20.370
in and out of their lungs.

00:01:20.370 --> 00:01:21.910
But turtles can't do this

00:01:21.910 --> 00:01:24.860
because their rigid shells don't expand.

00:01:24.860 --> 00:01:27.330
So instead they rely on sheets of muscles

00:01:27.330 --> 00:01:31.400
within their shell to pump in
oxygen through their mouths.

00:01:31.400 --> 00:01:33.970
That is, most of the time.

00:01:33.970 --> 00:01:35.890
Then there are other times

00:01:35.890 --> 00:01:39.040
when turtles breathe out the other end,

00:01:39.040 --> 00:01:42.560
more specifically, through what
scientists call the cloaca.

00:01:42.560 --> 00:01:46.300
It's the same opening that
turtles use to urinate,

00:01:46.300 --> 00:01:49.100
defecate, and lay eggs.

00:01:49.100 --> 00:01:52.800
And in some cases, it can
double as a set of gills,

00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:56.930
sucking in water and
absorbing the oxygen within.

00:01:56.930 --> 00:01:59.390
Scientists think that turtles do this

00:01:59.390 --> 00:02:02.550
when they're spending long
periods of time underwater,

00:02:02.550 --> 00:02:04.340
like when they're hibernating.

00:02:04.340 --> 00:02:07.870
And if you look really closely
at the inside of a shell,

00:02:07.870 --> 00:02:09.770
you'd discover another feature

00:02:09.770 --> 00:02:12.660
that helps with hibernating underwater:

00:02:12.660 --> 00:02:15.240
a scaffold-like structure that can store

00:02:15.240 --> 00:02:17.230
and release chemicals.

00:02:17.230 --> 00:02:19.810
That structure actually
helps turtles breathe

00:02:19.810 --> 00:02:22.070
without any oxygen at all.

00:02:22.070 --> 00:02:23.850
It works like this:

00:02:23.850 --> 00:02:26.540
Many turtles hibernate in frozen ponds

00:02:26.540 --> 00:02:28.690
that are starved of oxygen,

00:02:28.690 --> 00:02:31.700
and to survive, their
metabolism switches over

00:02:31.700 --> 00:02:34.220
from aerobic to anaerobic.

00:02:34.220 --> 00:02:36.930
That means they stop
using oxygen for energy

00:02:36.930 --> 00:02:39.170
and start using glucose instead

00:02:39.170 --> 00:02:42.840
via a process called
anaerobic respiration.

00:02:42.840 --> 00:02:46.310
And the byproduct of that is lactic acid.

00:02:46.310 --> 00:02:48.980
Now, theoretically,
this acid could build up

00:02:48.980 --> 00:02:51.420
in a turtle's body and kill it.

00:02:51.420 --> 00:02:54.310
That's where the shell's
structure comes in.

00:02:54.310 --> 00:02:56.530
It can absorb the lactic acid

00:02:56.530 --> 00:03:00.710
as well as release a bicarbonate
to neutralize that acid.

00:03:00.710 --> 00:03:04.330
It's essentially Tums, but for turtles.

00:03:04.330 --> 00:03:07.920
So as it turns out, having
a shell is pretty handy

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:10.220
for certain situations.

00:03:10.220 --> 00:03:12.600
In fact, scientists think that turtles

00:03:12.600 --> 00:03:15.160
originally got their shells for digging,

00:03:15.160 --> 00:03:19.180
likely more than 200 million years ago.

00:03:19.180 --> 00:03:21.720
Wojakowski: They dig, like,
really, really complex

00:03:21.720 --> 00:03:23.630
burrowing structures underground.

00:03:23.630 --> 00:03:26.410
[Narrator] And of course,
shells are incredibly useful

00:03:26.410 --> 00:03:28.930
for defense against predators,

00:03:28.930 --> 00:03:31.218
no matter how fierce they may be.

00:03:32.853 --> 00:03:35.137
Turtles are amazing.

